dp-service — Release Artifacts and Verification
===============================================

This repository publishes the dp-service backend as a self-contained executable JAR
via GitHub Releases.

Each release corresponds to a Git tag of the form:

  rel-<version>

Example:
  rel-1.12.0-rc8

------------------------------------------------------------
Release Contents
------------------------------------------------------------

Each release contains:

  dp-service-<version>.jar
    Executable service JAR (fat / shaded).

  SHA256SUMS
    SHA-256 checksum for the JAR above, in sha256sum format.

  SHA256SUMS.cosign.bundle
    A keyless Sigstore signature over SHA256SUMS.

Releases through 1.16.0 instead shipped a separate dp-service-<version>.jar.sha256,
and were not signed.  That file recorded the path as release/dp-service-<version>.jar,
so `sha256sum -c` fails on it in a flat download directory unless a release/
subdirectory is recreated.  The verification steps below do not apply to those
releases.

The container image ghcr.io/osprey-dcs/dp-service is published from the same
tag and signed separately; see "Container Image" below.

------------------------------------------------------------
Download and Installation
------------------------------------------------------------

1. Download the JAR, SHA256SUMS, and SHA256SUMS.cosign.bundle from the GitHub
   Release page into a single directory, with no subdirectories.  Both commands
   below expect to find the files beside SHA256SUMS.

2. Verify the checksum and the signature.

   a. The checksum:

        sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS

      (On macOS, which has no sha256sum: shasum -a 256 -c SHA256SUMS)

      The output should indicate:

        dp-service-<version>.jar: OK

      SHA256SUMS lists only the JAR, so there is nothing to skip with
      --ignore-missing.  It does not list itself or SHA256SUMS.cosign.bundle, so
      this step says nothing about either file; the signature check below is what
      covers them.

   b. The signature.

      The checksum establishes integrity but not origin: it is published to the
      same release page as the JAR, so anyone able to replace the JAR could
      replace its checksum alongside it.  The Sigstore signature closes that gap
      by binding SHA256SUMS to the repository, workflow file, tag, and source
      commit that produced it.  There is no public key to fetch or trust: the
      signing identity is a short-lived certificate issued to the GitHub Actions
      run and recorded in the public Rekor transparency log.

      Install cosign (the signatures are produced with cosign v3.0.6):

        https://docs.sigstore.dev/cosign/system_config/installation/

      Then, with <version> replaced by the release you downloaded (e.g. 1.17.0):

        cosign verify-blob \
          --bundle SHA256SUMS.cosign.bundle \
          --certificate-identity 'https://github.com/osprey-dcs/dp-service/.github/workflows/release.yml@refs/tags/rel-<version>' \
          --certificate-oidc-issuer https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com \
          --certificate-github-workflow-trigger push \
          SHA256SUMS

      The output should be:

        Verified OK

      Keep every identity flag exactly as written; each one closes a specific gap:

        --certificate-identity  is an exact match on this repository, the
            release.yml workflow file, and the one tag you are verifying.  A
            pattern matching any rel- tag would accept an older release's genuine
            SHA256SUMS and bundle substituted for this one's.  A loosened or
            unanchored pattern would accept a valid Sigstore signature made by any
            workflow in any repository -- the usual way this check is made vacuous
            while still appearing to pass.

        --certificate-github-workflow-trigger push  requires the signature to come
            from the tag push that published the release, not from a manual run of
            the same workflow, which would carry the same identity.

      The identity is specific to the JAR: it is not the identity for the
      container image, which is built and signed by a different workflow file.

3. Run the service:

   java -jar dp-service-<version>.jar

------------------------------------------------------------
Container Image
------------------------------------------------------------

Each release also publishes ghcr.io/osprey-dcs/dp-service, tagged rel-<version>
and latest, with a keyless Sigstore signature on the image digest.  The image is
a separate build of the same tagged source: it does not contain the JAR above, so
the two signatures attest two builds.  Images published before signing began
(through 1.16.0) have no signature.

The package is private to the osprey-dcs organization: pulling or verifying the
image needs a GitHub account with read access to it.  Log in first, with a
personal access token that has the read:packages scope:

     echo "$TOKEN" | docker login ghcr.io -u <github-user> --password-stdin

cosign uses the same Docker credentials, so both steps below then work.  Without
the login, both fail with a 401 (UNAUTHORIZED) -- an access error, not a missing
or invalid signature.  The JAR on the release page needs no login.

1. Pull by tag or by digest:

     docker pull ghcr.io/osprey-dcs/dp-service:rel-<version>
     docker pull ghcr.io/osprey-dcs/dp-service@sha256:<digest>

   A tag is mutable and a digest is not.  Deploying by the digest you verified
   is the only way to run exactly what was verified.

2. Verify the signature, with <version> replaced by the release (e.g. 1.17.0).
   This needs cosign v3 or later.  The signature is stored as an OCI referrer (a
   Sigstore bundle attached to the image digest), which cosign v2 does not look
   for: cosign v2.4 and v2.5 report "no signatures found" against a correctly
   signed image.  Seeing that error, check the cosign version before anything else.

     cosign verify \
       --certificate-identity 'https://github.com/osprey-dcs/dp-service/.github/workflows/release-image.yml@refs/tags/rel-<version>' \
       --certificate-oidc-issuer https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com \
       --certificate-github-workflow-trigger push \
       ghcr.io/osprey-dcs/dp-service:rel-<version>

   cosign resolves the tag to a digest and verifies that digest; the output names
   it, and a successful check prints the verified signature payload as JSON.
   To verify the exact image you will deploy, give the digest instead of the tag,
   with the same flags:

     cosign verify \
       --certificate-identity 'https://github.com/osprey-dcs/dp-service/.github/workflows/release-image.yml@refs/tags/rel-<version>' \
       --certificate-oidc-issuer https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com \
       --certificate-github-workflow-trigger push \
       ghcr.io/osprey-dcs/dp-service@sha256:<digest>

   The identity names release-image.yml, not release.yml: the image is signed by
   a different workflow file than the JAR, and the JAR's command above fails
   against the image, as this one fails against the JAR.  Keep both identity
   flags exactly as written, for the reasons given for the JAR.

   Verifying :latest verifies whatever :latest points at now.  It moves on every
   release, so a later pull of :latest may be a different image; verify and deploy
   by digest.

------------------------------------------------------------
Configuration
------------------------------------------------------------

Runtime configuration is provided via external files, typically:

  application.yml
  log4j2.xml

These are supplied by the data-platform installer or deployment environment.

------------------------------------------------------------
Notes
------------------------------------------------------------

- dp-service depends on dp-grpc APIs.
- The service is designed to be run as part of the Data Platform distribution.
